=== Graphical Interfaces
tag::concept[]

(((GUIs)))(((Graphical tools)))
While the terminal is Git’s native environment and where new features appear first, it isn't always the best choice for every task or every designer. Sometimes a visual representation of your history or a simple point-and-click interface for staging changes is exactly what you need.

At SketchSpark, the team uses a mix of tools. Nora (UX Lead) handles most things in the terminal, but Priya (Illustrator) and Sam (UI Designer) often reach for graphical tools to manage their assets.

#### GitHub Desktop
tag::procedure[]

(((GitHub Desktop)))
GitHub Desktop is the primary visual client for the team. It is designed to simplify the "GitHub Flow" and focuses on the most common tasks: cloning, branching, committing, and syncing.

.GitHub Desktop interface
image::images/github_mac.png[GitHub Desktop]

**Key Features for Designers:**
*   **Visual Diffing:** Just like the GitHub website, the desktop app allows you to see visual changes in PNGs and other images.
*   **Simple Syncing:** Instead of remembering `fetch`, `pull`, and `push`, you just click the **Sync** button. It handles the network dance for you.
*   **Easy Staging:** You can click checkboxes next to modified files to stage them, making it easy to create atomic commits without typing filenames.

GitHub Desktop is "evergreen"—it updates itself in the background and includes its own bundled version of Git, so Priya never has to worry about manual installations.

#### Gitk and Git-GUI
tag::reference[]

(((gitk)))(((git gui)))
If you’ve installed Git, you already have two simple visual tools: `gitk` (for viewing history) and `git-gui` (for crafting commits). They are more task-oriented and less polished than GitHub Desktop, but they are available on every system where Git is installed.

To see a graphical view of your project's branches and merges, Nora often runs:
[source,bash]
----
$ gitk --all
----

#### Other GUI Options
tag::reference[]

There are dozens of other Git clients, ranging from the powerful (Sourcetree, GitKraken) to the minimal. If GitHub Desktop doesn't fit your workflow, the official Git website maintains a curated list at https://git-scm.com/downloads/guis[^].
